McMaster was controlled by the Baptist Convention of Ontario and Quebec until it became a privately chartered, publicly funded non-denominational institution in 1957.
McMaster University is particularly well known for its medical school, which was recently ranked 25th in the world and 3rd in Canada by the Times Higher Education World University Subject Rankings in 2015.
It was founded in 1881 as Toronto Baptist College.
As the university grew, McMaster Hall started to become overcrowded.
The university nearly became federated with the University of Toronto, as had been the case with Trinity College and Victoria College.
Professional programs during the interwar period were limited to just theology and nursing.
During the Second World War and post-war periods the demand for technological expertise, particularly in the sciences, increased.
In particular, the institution could no longer secure sufficient funds from denominational sources alone to sustain science research.
The arts and divinity programs were reconstituted as University College and science was reorganized under the newly incorporated Hamilton College as a separate division capable of receiving provincial grants.
The University traditionally focused on undergraduate studies, and did not offer a PhD program until 1949.
In 1950, the university had completed the construction of three academic buildings for the sciences, all designed by local architect William Russell Souter.
The historic Baptist connection was continued through McMaster Divinity College, a separately chartered affiliated college of the university.
In 1968 the university was reorganized under an amended act of the McMaster Act into the Divisions of Arts, Science, and Health Sciences, each with its own Vice-President, while the Divinity College continued under its existing arrangement.
The Faculties of Business, Engineering, Health Sciences, Humanities, Science, and Social Sciences were retained, each under the leadership of a dean.
Currently, the university comprises 11 faculties, seven graduate schools, a university affiliated institute, a national joint-use facility, a university hospital, and an affiliate school.
“Creation of advanced intelligence and succession of exact intelligence” is the principle of the university.
