Scottish students in the middle ages were forced abroad to pursue their studies, with no national university to develop their academic abilities.
So when the Catholic church was divided by two rival popes — with Pope Boniface IX supported by the French cardinals while Scotland remained faithful to Pope Benedict XIII — Scottish students found themselves in a difficult position.
St Andrews was the obvious choice — the seat of the greatest bishopric in Scotland and location of a monastery noted as a centre for learning.
This granted the masters and students recognition as a properly constituted corporation, duly privileged and safeguarded for the pursuit of learning.
In return for Scotland’s loyalty, Pope Benedict readily agreed and on 28 August 1413 full University status was conferred by a series of six papal bulls – one of which survives to this day in the University of St Andrews museum, MUSA.
They arrived in the town in February 1414 — to be welcomed with bells, bonfires, and great celebration.
Although the text of all six is known, only the confirmation of Wardlaw’s grant survives in the original and still bears its bulla or lead seal.
In 1544 the University banned beards, the carrying of weapons, gambling and football.
The buildings of St Mary’s College and St Salvator’s Chapel date from this period.
During this time St Salvator’s and St Leonard’s Colleges joined to form the United College which still survives today in a greatly enlarged form.
In 1897 the University was joined by a new academic centre in nearby Dundee and with it gained notable achievements in medical and applied science.
In the 1980s, St Andrews embarked on a broad programme of investment to boost its research capabilities, a strategy which has helped establish its reputation today as an international centre of research excellence.
International scholars have been coming to St Andrews to study, teachers to teach, and students to learn since the foundation in 1413.
