In 1957, innovation and entrepreneurship brought University of Waterloo into being, as a group of business leaders imagined a new university built to tackle some of the world’s most daunting challenges.
Discoveries in science, medicine and engineering were coming fast and furious.
Waterloo was built to teach people to think in new ways.
It meant working hand-in-hand with industry, letting people own their intellectual property and the success that came from commercialization.
A chemical engineering building was the first to rise in 1958, followed by a physics and mathematics building a year later.
In 1967, Waterloo became home to the country’s only English-language school of optometry.
That spirit of risk-taking and innovation caught fire with students and researchers alike, helping to define this region’s enduring global identity as a technology powerhouse.
