Stay another day in this up and coming destination, as Banteay Srei offers some of the most accessible homestays in Cambodia, ranging from traditional village homestays to boutique homes. There are also lots of local restaurants in the district serving traditional Cambodian food, including the famous nam ben chok noodles.
Cambodia’s tradition of artisanship is exemplified by the incredible bas-relief carvings on the Temples of Angkor and its intricate statuary. There are several handicraft enterprises in Banteay Srei District...
Food is a major feature in this trip, including the production of food on local farms and the preparation of food with a Cambodian cooking class. Visits include learning about sugar palm cultivation and rice cultivation, as well as trying local delicacy nam ben chok.
* Price ranges are based on distance and numbers, as some destinations like Preah Dak Village and Banteay Samre Temple are only 15-20km from Siem Reap, while destinations such as Kbal Spean are 50km away.
Cambodians are a conservative people and what’s acceptable back home may be inappropriate here
While shorts and a singlet are acceptable clothing at the beach they are not suitable in the city or countryside, despite the heat of the day, so try to dress modestly. Upper arms and legs should be covered, especially at religious sites such as Banteay Srei temple.
Khmers greet one another with a sompiah, holding both hands together and bowing slightly. To show more respect, move your hands higher and bow lower.
When entering a religious site or someone’s house (and many places of business), take off your shoes and leave them outside.
Don’t criticise others, even if they are in the wrong and especially not in public or they will ‘lose face’. Just smile through gritted teeth and offer a sompiah.
Check with a host family what time electricity is provided as it may not be 24 hours in rural areas. Do not leave electric lights on over night as electricity is expensive.
Business Name : Dragon farm and homestay
Business Name : Cambodia Landmine Museum
Business Name : The Angkor Centre for Conservation of Biodiversity (ACCB)
A homestay is an immersive way to learn more about local life in Cambodia by staying with a family. Banteay Srei District is the most convenient place to experience a homestay in the kingdom as it lies just 25km from Siem Reap.
Green Homestay Is A Small Farm With A Big Heart, Offering A Couple Of Rooms For Guests And A Large Outdoor Area For Camping. Fresh Farm Products Are Available And Camping Is Available For School Trips Or Teambuilding.
After lunch at a local restaurant, we head east to Phnom Bok to visit a hilltop temple and take in the views over the district. After a hot climb, there is the option to relax at Ta Neav Reservoir.
On the second day, start with an early morning visit to Banteay Srei to beat the crowds and then enjoy a short boat trip on the ancient baray of the temple. Return to the homestay to learn about life in the local village, including a walk through the rice fields, a ride on an ox cart, a visit to a local farm and the chance to learn how to prepare some traditional Cambodian dishes like nam ben chok.
There are many restaurants in Banteay Srei District, including everything from large tourist-style restaurants that can cater to tour groups to local food stalls serving authentic local specialties. Some restaurants can also offer traditional cooking classes for those interested in Khmer cuisine.
This is an educational themed tour aimed at school groups and families wanting their children to learn more about local life. Leaving Siem Reap, travel to Banteay Srei District to visit the community.
For those without a temple pass, an alternative option is to visit the nearby hilltop pagoda on Phnom Hop. After a local lunch, continue to the Banteay Srei Butterfly Centre (BBC) to learn about the many varieties of butterfly in Siem Reap.
On the second day, there is the option of a peaceful early morning visit to the temple of Banteay Srei to beat the crowds. Make an adventurous trip to the Kbal Teuk Community Forest to explore this diverse forest environment with the chance to see some rare flora and fauna like the carnivorous pitcher plants that are found here.
However, Banteay Srei is so much more than a temple, offering visitors a diverse range of real-life experiences just a short distance from cosmopolitan Siem Reap. Discover life beyond the temples and experience the real Cambodia with a visit to the heart of Banteay Srei District.
The most popular walk in Banteay Srei District is currently the trek up to Kbal Spean and the ‘River of a Thousand Lingas’. However, village walks are also growing in popularity around homestay centers...
Banteay Srei District is made up of living, breathing communities engaged in traditional agricultural lifestyles which can be a fascinating glimpse of local life for visitors from non-tropical countries...
Headline attractions include the temples of Banteay Srei and Banteay Samre, as well as popular locations such as the Cambodia Landmine Museum and the Banteay Srei Butterfly Museum. There is also the chance to experience local life with an ox cart ride and a farm visit.
